  "text": "In order to solve this problem, we need to understand that the numbers are represented in a certain base $b$ and that the letters $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, and so on represent the digits $0$, $1$, $2$, $3$, and so on up to $b-1$ in base $b$. \n\nThe numbers in the equation are:\n\n$$\\begin{matrix} \\qquad \\qquad & \\qquad \\qquad & \\qquad \\qquad & \\qquad \\qquad & \\qquad \\qquad & b^3 \\\\ & b^2 & b & & & \\\\ b & & & \\qquad & & \\end{matrix}$$\n\nAdding the numbers in this column, we get:\n\n$$\\begin{matrix} b^3 & b^2 & b & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ + & b^2 & b & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\\\hline b^3 & b^2 & b & 1 & 0 & 0 \\end{matrix}$$\n\nThe resulting number is a three-digit number with $1$ as the hundreds place digit, $0$ as the tens place digit, and so on. \n\nThe equation can be re-written as follows:\n\n$$b^{4-1}A + b^{4-2}B + b^{4-3}C = \\overline{EFGHI} _b$$\n\nWhere $\\overline{EFGHI} _b$ represents the resulting number in base $b$. \n\nSince we know that $100 _b$ represents a three-digit number with $0$ as the hundreds place digit and $0$ as the tens place digit, we can deduce that $100 _b = b$ in base $b$. \n\nBy subtracting $b^{4-3}C$ from both sides of the equation (where $C$ represents $0,b-1$), we get:\n\n$$b^{4-1}A + b^{4-2}B = \\overline{EFGHI} _b - b^{4-3}C$$\n\nSince $\\overline{EFGHI} _b - b^{4-3}C$ must be a multiple of $b$, and the highest place digit in $A$ and $B$ is $b-1$, the only possible solution for this equation is when $b = 4$.\n\nThus, the value of $b$ is $\\boxed{4}$.",
